  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Agriculture obligated $7.3B in contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department awarded 20,825 contracts with an average award size of $349K. Obligations fell 24.1% year over year. The current-year total is partial because fiscal year to date figures are reported. Agriculture's spending has trended down from prior fiscal peaks: FY24 obligations were $11.6B and FY23 obligations were $11.5B. The Department continues to buy a mix of transportation, construction, food processing, forestry support, and IT services, with the largest NAICS categories including Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation at $802M and Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction at $452M. The top vendors reflect that mix, with KIEWIT INFRASTRUCTURE SOUTH CO holding $261M in obligations and food processors and specialty suppliers like TYSON FOODS INC. and PALANTIR TECHNOLOGIES INC. also appearing among top recipients. These figures describe federal contract obligations reported through FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What are Agriculture's largest spending categories right now?",
        "body": "The Department of Agriculture is spending heavily on transportation and food manufacturing categories. Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation accounts for $802M and Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction accounts for $452M. Fruit and Vegetable Canning and Animal (except Poultry) Slaughtering follow with $442M and $433M respectively. Support Activities for Forestry and Poultry Processing also rank high at $400M and $395M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest contractors to Agriculture this fiscal year?",
        "body": "The Department of Agriculture obligated major contracts to a mix of construction, food, and tech firms. KIEWIT INFRASTRUCTURE SOUTH CO received $261M and TYSON FOODS INC. received $176M. PALANTIR TECHNOLOGIES INC. received $129M while HELICOPTER TRANSPORT SERVICES, LLC and PACIFIC COAST PRODUCERS received $116M and $109M respectively. Multiple food processors and IT resellers appear in the top ten, showing diverse vendor demand."
      }
    ]
  },
